            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> * `is_enabled() == true` - means that the capability is enabled _in general_, the following (more precise) queries can override it;\r\n\r\nmakes sense. although the vim options system has taught users/plugins to care about the \"current context\" (buf/win/lsp-client/...), that probably doesn't make sense for LSP capabilities.\r\n\r\n> * `is_enabled({ client_id }) == true` - the capability is enabled for the client in any buffer, however individual buffers can restrict this;\r\n> * `is_enabled({ bufnr }) == true` - the capability is allowed in the buffer for any enabled client;\r\n> * `is_enabled({ client_id, bufnr }) == true` - allowed for the client in this buffer, this one can override per client/per buffer state.\r\n\r\ni'm confused by \"enabled\" vs \"allowed\", is that an intentional distinction?\r\n\r\nbtw, can you add an LSP section to `dev_arch.txt` somewhere, outlining this? we can keep iterating on it.",
