        },
        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "> i'm confused by \"enabled\" vs \"allowed\", is that an intentional distinction?\r\n\r\nNot really intentional. It's just that the term \"enabled\" rather means \"allowed, available and thus applied\". When querying for a buffer only `is_enabled` rather means that the buffer is not explicitly prohibited, but not only there might be disabled clients, there might not be applicable clients at all.\r\n\r\nSo I used \"allowed\" as it seemed more to the point.\r\n\r\n> btw, can you add an LSP section to dev_arch.txt somewhere, outlining this? we can keep iterating on it.\r\n\r\nSure thing.\r\n\r\nAlso to better frame the following conversations:\r\nFiltering by client name and reworking folding range are definitely outside the scope here and if everyone agrees by the time this is merged, there should be open issues for that.\r\n\r\nBut what about using `nil` value to disable overrides? It doesn't break any tests and IMO fits into this PR.\r\nSo nice concise statement `something.enable()` will still work as before, but when needed, an explicit override can be removed.",
            "created_at": "2026-07-20T14:10:03Z",
